A||N||24||Xtreme Fiero's||what is the weak link in a duke?||||1||
Z||000000||Xtreme Fiero's||10-06-2000||09:42 PM||||<BR>I want to bild a good/geat 2.5-2.7L duke. what do NEED to upgrade? I plan on geting billet rods & JE pistons, is there anything else? Crank, Block or head? I also plan on geting the Fiero store hi-pro cam and 1.6 roller rockers. can a stock crank take 6000 rpm, I think thats a good limit. I just don't want to dump a bunch of money into this and kill it in a month, I have a bad habit of doing that  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/redface.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Ozzy||10-06-2000||10:00 PM||||Let me repeat what everyone told me...<P><B>forgedaboutit!</B><P>I had a nice '88 Coupe and luved it but the "Duke" had an obvious lack of balls. I asked the same question everyone else has...what does this engine need. The reply is always " A different engine"!  Especially if you are planning on dumping a bunch of bucks on a complete rebuild, do a swap. <P>I'm no expert here; I'm repeating what our "in house" experts have said in the past and this question comes up periodically as new members join.<P>The crank and block are weak 'cause this was the GM econo engine. Built to a price...a cheap price! <P>Folks have stood fast here as well...something like this; "I don't believe it! I'm gonna slap a turbo on and do this and do that etc, etc, etc. To date, I don't believe anyone has done it. The reality becomes bigger than the performance gained/wallet ratio.<P>Sorry to burst yo bubble. Persist if you wan't...Don't shoot the messenger!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> I bought a GT instead!<P>Ozzy||||reg||11
Z||000002||Xtreme Fiero's||10-06-2000||10:14 PM||||oh well I guess i'll drop $15,000 for a billet block & head. and mabye 2G more for a billet crank.<P> LOL NOT!!!!!<BR> <BR>i'll just go with a great stock motor. The plan is to sell it for a 85 GT anyway   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  <p>[This message has been edited by Xtreme Fiero's (edited 10-06-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||WKDFIRO||10-07-2000||03:27 PM||||I was not nesessarily one of those people who wanted to beat GTs with my 4 cyl but when I "beefed" up my 87 5 speed coupe I got noticed. <P>      I started with trashing that good for nothing exhaust manifold and went to the Hooker Tri-Y header, some how lost the catalytic converter and put a straight pipe in place of the water trap with K&N filter. <P>Originally I wanted to beef it up a little just so I wouldn't bog the car down with 225/50/16 tires. What I got was a Fiero that wasn't left in the dust by the GTs. When I would go on long cruises with my club the GTs would be filling up with gas and I would be topping off! I think the best gas mileage I pulled out of that engine was 350 miles on a single tank. (ALL highway! Loved that 5th gear!!!)||||reg||1
Z||000004||SloPonti||10-07-2000||05:50 PM||||there is nothing wrong with hopping up the iron duck! i did mine about a year ago and everyone told me it will break and it is not worth it! they were wrong! i bored out the motor .40 over and redid all valve guides and the head was also redone! i did a new crank new rods and new ARP rod bolts! new ARP main cap bolt! hooker tri y header! gt dual exhaust! holley throttle body! ram air sidescoop with direct flow into the throtle body! the only draw back on my car is the automatic! but is does great enough for me! when i auto cross the car i am only a second or two behind the v8 firebirds camaros ss and the mustangs! not bad for a four cylider!! the car runs great and sounds great!!||||reg||1
Z||000005||batboy||10-07-2000||08:58 PM||||The "Iron Duke" seems to be a good reliable engine, as long as it's not abused regularly. But, for a mild warm-up, definitely go with headers and free flowing exhaust. That's the best power gains you can make without sacrificing a lot of dependability. Still, I would keep the RPMs down for longevity. The 2.5 usually drops out of it's power peak after about 4,500 RPM anyway, unless you made a hipo cam change. Rods sometimes appear to be a bit of a weak point on the Duke. Always replace the timing chain and gears when you have a 2.5 apart. Another good power maker is done by spending hard earned money and letting the machine shop do a custom head rebuild and pocket porting. Lots of extra flow and power can be found there, but it's not real cheap. However, for the amount of money spent, the power returns are not all that great on the 2.5 motor.||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||10-07-2000||09:42 PM||||The motor was made to be cheap. It all depends what you intend to do with it. I have a suspicion it depends what year block it is too. The later blocks may be more reliable than the older ones.<P>For pre 87's I'm told the S10 block is stronger but after looking at one externally you'd have to fool with all kinds of things. I can't confirm the block is any beter. ((I think all DIS blocks are the same. Cost to much to make diferant versions unless you have a real good reason. Adding DIS required redesigning the block as it is.))<P>You can't use the S10 head, no waterpump mount. The S10 pump is in the normal spot. (This confirmed what I suspected the cover on the front of the Fiero block is doing.)<P>||||reg||1
Z||000007||Xtreme Fiero's||10-07-2000||11:35 PM||||here is the plan:<P>84' Block & head bored .40 over & line honed, Fresh head hi-pro valve job & gasket matched.<P>new crank (stock)<BR>new rods (stock)<BR>Hyper pistons <BR>Fiero store hi-pro 84' cam<BR>1.6 roller rockers  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>Holley dominator intake (ported)  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>Holley 300cfm TBI  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>custom header & dual exhaust  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>ARP rod/main studs<BR>ARP head studs<BR>(ARP bolts where available)<BR>85-86 ECM w/ chip <BR>stock 4:10 4-speed <BR>centerforce clutch (Gold)<P>My goal is to run with most GT's in the 1/4 mi. mabye a 5.0 mud-stain (stock)  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> what that a mid 15? i'm hopeing for 140 hp @ the crank so I should get 120 out of it  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||88formula||10-08-2000||12:59 AM||||What a shame. That motor is a piece of junk. I've owned one of them and my brother owned one also. I had nothing but problems with them and they never ran right. Both of them had a skipping problem that no one could figure out. If you want reliable performance than get a v6. My v6 formula 5-speed gets better gas mileage (best of 30 mpg) than the 87 automatic fiero coupe I had in the past (best of 25 mpg).||||reg||1
Z||000009||baldlobo||10-08-2000||01:49 AM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 88formula:<BR><B>What a shame. That motor is a piece of junk. I've owned one of them and my brother owned one also. I had nothing but problems with them and they never ran right. Both of them had a skipping problem that no one could figure out. If you want reliable performance than get a v6. My v6 formula 5-speed gets better gas mileage (best of 30 mpg) than the 87 automatic fiero coupe I had in the past (best of 25 mpg).</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>stop biteing his head off you know my stand on the subject. I like my duke   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>ps. could you please look at <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/005565.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/005565.html</A>   and see if you can dyno it for me, 88formula; It's the last one on the page.<p>[This message has been edited by baldlobo (edited 10-08-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||88formula||10-08-2000||12:33 PM||||If you want me to give you 0-60 times and 1/4 mile times you must give me all your vehicle information like which transmission you have, vehicle weight, tire size & kind, final drive gear ratio, and your weight (160 lbs. right). <P>Here are the dynos I got starting with the baseline stock motor so you have something to compare the gains too.<P>Stock motor.<BR> 96 hp @ 4000 rpm & 136 ft. lbs. of torque @ 3000 rpm.<P>Stock motor with just headers.<BR>113 hp @ 4000 rpm & 153 ft. lbs. of torque @ 3500 rpm<P>Stock motor with header and ported heads.<BR>132 hp @ 5000 rpm & 159 ft. lbs. of torque @ 3500-4000 rpm.<P>Stock motor with just Lunati cam.<BR>139 hp @ 5500 rpm & 143 ft. lbs. of torque @ 4500 rpm.<P>Lunati cam with headers and nothing else.<BR>164 hp @ 5500 rpm & 167 ft. lbs. of torque @ 4500 rpm<P>Lunati cam with headers & ported heads.<BR>192 hp @ 6500 rpm & 177 ft. lbs. of torque @ 5000 rpm with not bottom-end power.<P>You will need an iron duke with a stronger block and bottom end to sustain those kinds of figures especially if you want to rev it, probably super duty duke parts$$$$ <P>All these dynos assume perfect air, spark, and  fuel ratios for any given combination. <P>PS, a modified 3.4-liter Camaro engine will give you 209 hp @ 5500 rpm & 237 ft lbs. of torque @ 4000 rpm. This will propel a 2925 lb. fiero to 60 mph in 5.2 seconds and go through the quarter mile in 13.6 seconds. This is if you launch @ 3500 rpm and shift @ 6000 rpm with the 5-speed transmission and a 3.61 axle ratio. You will also have lots of bottom-end torque along with more reliability.<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000011||baldlobo||10-08-2000||01:37 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 88formula:<BR><B>If you want me to give you 0-60 times and 1/4 mile times you must give me all your vehicle information like which transmission you have, vehicle weight, tire size & kind, final drive gear ratio, and your weight (160 lbs. right). <P>Here are the dynos I got starting with the baseline stock motor so you have something to compare the gains too.<P>Stock motor.<BR> 96 hp @ 4000 rpm & 136 ft. lbs. of torque @ 3000 rpm.<P>Stock motor with just headers.<BR>113 hp @ 4000 rpm & 153 ft. lbs. of torque @ 3500 rpm<P>Stock motor with header and ported heads.<BR>132 hp @ 5000 rpm & 159 ft. lbs. of torque @ 3500-4000 rpm.<P>Stock motor with just Lunati cam.<BR>139 hp @ 5500 rpm & 143 ft. lbs. of torque @ 4500 rpm.<P>Lunati cam with headers and nothing else.<BR>164 hp @ 5500 rpm & 167 ft. lbs. of torque @ 4500 rpm<P>Lunati cam with headers & ported heads.<BR>192 hp @ 6500 rpm & 177 ft. lbs. of torque @ 5000 rpm with not bottom-end power.<P>You will need an iron duke with a stronger block and bottom end to sustain those kinds of figures especially if you want to rev it, probably super duty duke parts$$$$ <P>All these dynos assume perfect air, spark, and  fuel ratios for any given combination. <P>PS, a modified 3.4-liter Camaro engine will give you 209 hp @ 5500 rpm & 237 ft lbs. of torque @ 4000 rpm. This will propel a 2925 lb. fiero to 60 mph in 5.2 seconds and go through the quarter mile in 13.6 seconds. This is if you launch @ 3500 rpm and shift @ 6000 rpm with the 5-speed transmission and a 3.61 axle ratio. You will also have lots of bottom-end torque along with more reliability.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Thanks, I'll get you that info sometime today(I've got alot of things to do today, so it might be tonight).||||reg||1
Z||000012||StuGood||10-08-2000||01:43 PM||||How do you plan to fit an "84' Block & head" into your car, when the whole Fiero is only about 4' wide?!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> Hehehe   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">    <P>Sounds like the weak link in a Duke is the engine    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">.<P>On the other hand, one could also say since it has timing <B>gears</B> (instead of a timing chain like the V6 has), the Duke <B>has</B> no weak link    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">! <BR><p>[This message has been edited by StuGood (edited 10-08-2000).]||||reg||12
Z||000013||Xtreme Fiero's||10-08-2000||07:16 PM||||<BR>88Formula: what would my car run in the 1/4 mile with a Header, Lunati cam and a ported head/intake. with the 4:10 4-speed, 225/40/16 Yokohama AVS tires and a 180lb driver. Thanks<P>P.S the car weights around 2400-2500 lb less the 180lb driver ||||reg||1
Z||000014||GT Bastard||10-08-2000||08:01 PM||||Good luck breaking into the 16 second quarter mile with a duck.  I'd be very impressed if you could pull it off. <P>Have fun though.||||reg||6
Z||000015||88formula||10-08-2000||09:11 PM||||Xtreme Fiero's, your 2730 lb. fiero (2550+180) will run the quarter mile in 13.9 seconds @ 95.9 mph and go 0-60 in 5.5 seconds if you launch @ 4500 rpm and shift @ 6825 rpm. This assumes you have a 7000-rpm reline. If you have a 5500-rpm reline, launch @ 4500 rpm, and shift @ 5500 rpm than your fiero will run the quarter mile in 14.7 seconds @ 89.2 mph and 0-60 in 6.4 seconds. <P>You will have no low –end power though.  For example, if you have a stock 2.5-liter fiero with the same drive train as above driving it the way you would most likely drive it on the street here is what you would get. If you launched @ 1500 rpm, shift 1-2 @ 3000 rpm, shift 2-3 @ 3500 rpm, and shift 3-4 @ 3000 rpm your fiero will reach 60 mph in 12 seconds and the ¼-mile in 18 seconds @ 78 mph. The 2.5 fiero with the Lunati camshaft, headers, and ported heads will do the same 0-60 in 13.6 seconds and the ¼-mile in 19 seconds @ 75 mph.   <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000016||Bob Englert||10-08-2000||09:52 PM||||I think the weak link (besides the overall tame performance) would have to be the phenolic timing gear used in the 2.5L.  These will break when you are very far from home on a Saturday evening when no one is available to pick you up.  <P>If you do a rebuild I would strongly suggest that you repalce the plastic gear with a metal timing gear.  Several vendors make these, some are listed for 84-86 motors only.  I think they can be used on the later ones but the valve timing mark is different in the 87-88 motors so a new crank gear might also be needed.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000017||Xtreme Fiero's||10-08-2000||10:38 PM||||How do I get low end power? I'm used to my import truck where the redling is 7300 RPM and not 5500 rpm. I could deal with a low 15 sec. 1/4 mile time if I got some toqure back. do I need to stroke it out?<P>Where can I get a "Stock" crank balanced and made ready for say a 7000 rpm redline? I like the sound of that 13.9 @ 95.9mph sounds cool   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> even a 14.6 would be kick ass   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>88Formula: what tf the same car was bored to 2.7, would that make any dif. in low end power<p>[This message has been edited by Xtreme Fiero's (edited 10-09-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000018||88formula||10-09-2000||05:02 PM||||Xtreme Fiero's, you must want to really be different at any cost. You can't bore your factory iron duke over .060" and I would not go over .030" because of the thin wall casting. To get 2.7-liters you have to get a stroked crankshaft from Pontiac, which is made for the Cosworth Pontiac "Iron Duke" 4-valve engine. If you did get the crankshaft it would make a big difference in low-end torque. To keep your low-end power don't get an aggressive camshaft. To get a Crankshaft able to sustain 7k you have to get the Cosworth Super Duty Block and crankshaft. The stock one is too weak. Here is a place that sells them <A HREF="http://www.gtsonline.com/krp/prod1.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.gtsonline.com/krp/prod1.htm</A>  . <A HREF="http://www.homestead.com/fiero2m4/index.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.homestead.com/fiero2m4/index.html</A> <BR> <BR>Here is a picture of one. <BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Cosworth_Iron_Duke.jpg">  <P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Cosworth_Iron_duke_back.jpg">  <BR>That engine will cost you about $10,000 to buy.<P><p>[This message has been edited by 88formula (edited 10-09-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000019||Xtreme Fiero's||10-09-2000||07:46 PM||||88Formula: no I don't need to be diffrent, I have decided to build the 2.5 "sotck" but "only" Bore it out .30 over and install hyper pistons a good cam, Holley TBI & Dominator Intake.  and ofcorse my custom exhaust w/ the stock manifold. I have to keep in mind that this car is for sale and I can't dump $10,000 into the motor, no one will buy it. so i'll just build a High quality duke.||||reg||1
Z||000020||baldlobo||10-09-2000||09:15 PM||||I'm wondering where the Dominator Intake is on the Holley website?<p>[This message has been edited by baldlobo (edited 10-09-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000021||batboy||10-09-2000||11:35 PM||||When Xtreme mentioned running a Duke at 7,000 RPM I laughed so hard I about peed my pants. I think 5 grand is about max if you want that poor 2.5 motor to have any longevity. Stroking it is out unless you have deep pockets. That requires custom pistons, unless I'm mistaken. You can either cam it and run the piss out of it at high RPM to make horsepower, but sacrifice low end torque, or you can make it breathe better (headers, porting, mild cam) like I mentioned in my earlier post and retain your bottom end torque<P>There is no replacement for displacement.||||reg||1
Z||000022||Standard||10-10-2000||04:26 AM||||I don't know about you guys, but I sure wouldn't mind having that red-headed thing in my GT.. or my 2m4 for that matter  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> &lt;sigh&gt; I can't wait 'till I win the lottery...<P>of course, an SD block, with side cover, main caps and main studs; fully machined, drilled and bored to specifications from KRP costs "only" $2,500  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> and a crankshaft from them is another $1,850... then again, it might be cheaper to piece together a performance 4 than to drop a V8 in.. hmmmmm.. <BR><P>------------------<BR>Mike -<BR>Black 86.5 GT 5 Spd, 151,000 miles and counting!         <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/BURNOUT.gif">      <BR>Red 84 Sport Coupe - winter donut maker   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/asthanos.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000023||fotofrank||10-10-2000||12:26 PM|||| The "dominator" input for a 2.5L is no longer made by Holley. To obtain better low end power the runners need to be longer on the input. Since no performance input is available put a "spacer" between the TB and the input. That efectively increses the runner length. The best header is a tri Y design and still available from Hooker(about $350 or so. A good exhaust is a grea idea but the lack of back pressure also decresses torque. Input and exhaust are a science all there own. A ported and polished head, good input and exhaust, Holley TBI and 2 degree or so of timing advance will purk up the DUKE and hopefully keep it from blowing up. If you are worried about the crank , one from a S-10 is a bit stronger than ours. It should fit OK. Amild cam , Ive heard, has the best bank for the buck in preformance. Good luck and keep us up to date on your engine mods. Remember the block isn't the best so boring is not the best way to go. Just a gues but the Holley TB,input and header should no cost more than $1100.00 if you can find an input.<P>------------------<BR>Peace and Happiness<BR>Frank and his fotoflash, an '84 SE 2M4<BR>http://fotofrank.homestead.com/foto1~ns4.html||||reg||12
Z||000024||mrossum||11-29-2000||09:53 AM||||i think most of you are wrong about the duke. i have a stroked one going together right now. have a crank out of early nova 153 that will go right in with an adapter for the rear main seal. cost of crank-$100. stock style pistone are used with crower 6" sportsman rods to get the compression height correct. total cost of this engine is in the $1200 - $1500 neighborhood, depending on the final bill for my head. will let you know how it turns out.||||reg||10
